# Module 5: Source to Silicon (The Blinky Paradigm)

**Level:** Intermediate · **Time:** ~75 min · **Hardware:** NUCLEO-F411RE

**Prerequisites:** Modules 1–4, basic digital logic (gates, flip-flops).

## Learning Objectives

By the end of this module you will be able to:

1. Describe the conceptual execution of an embedded application from reset to `main()` and into the main loop.
2. Compare the same operation as C, assembly and machine code.
3. Explain, step by step, what the MCU does internally when one instruction toggles a pin.

---

## 1. Conceptual Execution of an Embedded Application

```
Power-on / reset
   └─► Core loads SP and PC from the vector table
        └─► Reset handler (startup code)
             ├─ copy .data from flash to RAM
             ├─ zero .bss
             ├─ configure clocks (system init)
             └─ call main()
                  └─ initialize peripherals
                       └─ while (1) { do work }   ← never returns
```

An embedded application normally **never exits**. If `main()` returned there would be nothing to return to, so the main loop runs forever.

## 2. The Blinky in Three Representations

### 2.1 C

```c
#include <stdint.h>

#define RCC_AHB1ENR  (*(volatile uint32_t *)0x40023830U)
#define GPIOA_MODER  (*(volatile uint32_t *)0x40020000U)
#define GPIOA_BSRR   (*(volatile uint32_t *)0x40020018U)

int main(void)
{
    RCC_AHB1ENR |= 1U;                     /* clock GPIOA            */
    GPIOA_MODER &= ~(3U << 10);            /* PA5 mode bits = 00     */
    GPIOA_MODER |=  (1U << 10);            /* PA5 = output           */

    while (1) {
        GPIOA_BSRR = (1U << 5);            /* LED on                 */
        for (volatile uint32_t i = 0; i < 500000; i++) { }
        GPIOA_BSRR = (1U << (5 + 16));     /* LED off                */
        for (volatile uint32_t i = 0; i < 500000; i++) { }
    }
}
```

`volatile` on the loop counter stops the compiler from deleting the delay.

### 2.2 Assembly (Thumb-2)

To follow every instruction, reduce it to one action: turn the LED on, then stop.

```asm
    .syntax unified
    .thumb

start:
    ldr  r0, =0x40020018    @ r0 = address of GPIOA_BSRR
    movs r1, #0x20          @ r1 = 1 << 5  (bit for PA5)
    str  r1, [r0]           @ write r1 to [r0]: set PA5
    b    .                  @ branch to self: halt here
```

(Clock enable and pin mode setup omitted. Assume they are done.)

### 2.3 Machine code

When assembled, the four instructions (plus a stored 32-bit constant) look like this:

| Offset | Instruction | Encoding (hex) | Notes |
|---|---|---|---|
| 0x0 | `ldr r0, [pc, #4]` | `0x4801` | The `=0x40020018` pseudo-instruction becomes a PC-relative load from a *literal pool* |
| 0x2 | `movs r1, #0x20` | `0x2120` | `0010 0 001 00100000`: opcode, register 1, immediate |
| 0x4 | `str r1, [r0]` | `0x6001` | `0110 0 00000 000 001`: store, base r0, source r1 |
| 0x6 | `b .` | `0xE7FE` | Unconditional branch to itself |
| 0x8 | literal | `0x40020018` | The constant loaded by the first instruction |

In memory the bytes are **little-endian**:

```
01 48  20 21  01 60  FE E7  18 00 02 40
```

As an **Intel HEX** record (illustrative, placed at address 0):

```
:0C000000014820210160FEE718000240CA
```

| Field | Value | Meaning |
|---|---|---|
| `:` | | Start of record |
| `0C` | 12 | Byte count |
| `0000` | | Address |
| `00` | | Record type: data |
| `014820210160FEE718000240` | | The 12 data bytes |
| `CA` | | Checksum: two's complement of the sum of all other bytes |

A real image is placed at `0x08000000`, so the file begins with an *extended linear address* record: `:020000040800F2`.

### 2.4 Comparing the representations

| | C | Assembly | Machine code |
|---|---|---|---|
| Readable by | Humans | Humans (with effort) | The CPU |
| Portable | Mostly | No, tied to the ISA | No |
| Detail visible | Intent | Registers and instructions | Exact bits and addresses |
| Produced by | You | Compiler or you | Assembler |

